            "original": "The process for terminating a contract involves several steps, depending on the type of contract and the jurisdiction. Here's a general outline:\n\n**Unilateral Termination (One Party Closes the Deal)**\n\n1. **Notice**: The party terminating the contract (e.g., a buyer) must provide written notice to the other party (e.g., a seller) stating their intention to terminate the contract.\n2. **Reason for termination**: The notice must specify the reason for termination, such as non-payment or breach of contract.\n3. **Termination date**: The contract must specify a date by which the other party must terminate the contract.\n\n**Bilateral Termination (Both Parties Close the Deal)**\n\n1. **Notice**: Both parties must provide written notice to each other stating their intention to terminate the contract.\n2. **Reason for termination**: The notice must specify the reason for termination, such as non-payment or breach of contract.\n3. **Termination date**: The contract must specify a date by which both parties must terminate the contract.\n\n**Force Majeure (Unforeseen Circumstances)**\n\nIf an unforeseen circumstance (e.g., natural disaster, war, or pandemic) prevents either party from performing their obligations, the contract can be terminated under force majeure.\n\n**Other Termination Conditions**\n\n1. **Material breach**: If either party commits a material breach of contract, the other party can terminate the contract.\n2. **Change in ownership**: If the ownership of the contract changes (e.g., through sale or merger), the new owner may be able to terminate the contract.\n3. **Voluntary termination**: Either party can terminate the contract voluntarily, without notice or reason.\n\n**Consequences of Early Termination**\n\nEarly termination may have consequences, such as:\n\n1. **Loss of payment**: The terminated party may be liable for payment of any outstanding amounts.\n2. **Damage to reputation**: The terminated party may be subject to damage to their reputation.\n3. **Loss of business opportunities**: The terminated party may lose business opportunities or access to resources.\n\n**Notifying the Terminated Party**\n\nIt's essential to notify the terminated party of the termination date, reason for termination, and any other relevant details to avoid potential disputes or claims.\n\n**Reviewing the Contract**\n\nBefore terminating the contract, review it to ensure that it is still valid and enforceable. If the contract is invalid or unenforceable, the terminated party may be able to claim damages or seek injunctive relief.\n\nKeep in mind that the specific process and requirements for terminating a contract may vary depending on the jurisdiction, type of contract, and other factors.",
