            "original": "Avoiding drowning in unpaid bills requires a combination of financial planning, budgeting, and communication. Here are some steps you can take:\n\n1. **Create a budget**: Start by tracking your income and expenses to understand where your money is going. Make a budget that accounts for all your necessary expenses, savings, and debt repayment.\n2. **Prioritize your bills**: Make a list of all your bills, including credit card debt, loans, and utility bills. Prioritize them based on urgency and importance.\n3. **Pay more than the minimum**: Try to pay more than the minimum payment on your bills each month. This will help you pay off the principal balance faster and reduce the amount of interest you owe.\n4. **Consider debt consolidation**: If you have multiple bills with high interest rates, consider consolidating them into a single loan with a lower interest rate. This can simplify your payments and save you money on interest.\n5. **Communicate with your creditors**: If you're struggling to pay your bills, reach out to your creditors to explain your situation. They may be willing to work with you to set up a payment plan or temporarily reduce your payments.\n6. **Seek assistance**: If you're struggling to make ends meet, consider seeking assistance from a non-profit credit counseling agency or a financial advisor.\n7. **Use the 50/30/20 rule**: Allocate 50% of your income towards necessary expenses, 30% towards discretionary spending, and 20% towards saving and debt repayment.\n8. **Automate your payments**: Set up automatic payments for your bills to ensure you never miss a payment.\n9. **Monitor your credit report**: Check your credit report regularly to ensure it's accurate and up-to-date. Dispute any errors you find.\n10. **Take care of yourself**: Financial stress can affect your mental and physical health. Make sure to get enough sleep, exercise regularly, and eat a healthy diet.\n\nAdditionally, consider the following tips:\n\n- **Snow